Printf follows C format strings with Julia types: `%d` integers, `%f %e %g` floats, `%s` any value (via `string`), `%x %o` hex/octal, `%c` character, `%%` a literal percent sign. Flags are `-` (left-justify), `0` (zero pad), `+` (sign), and width/precision as in C. Binary (`%b`) is not supported; use `string(n, base=2)`.

```julia
using Printf

x = 3.14159; n = 42
specs = (@sprintf("%.2f", x), @sprintf("%5d|%-5d|%05d", n, n, n), @sprintf("%s and %s", "a", :b),
         @sprintf("%e", 12345.678), @sprintf("%x %o", 255, 8), @sprintf("%+d", n),
         @sprintf("%8s|%-8s|", "ab", "cd"), @sprintf("%5.1f%%", 12.345))

@printf("%-10s %6.2f\n", "total", 12.5)
for (k, v) in (("a", 1.5), ("bb", 22.25))
    @printf("%-4s|%8.3f|\n", k, v)
end
specs
```

It prints three lines (`total       12.50`, `a   |   1.500|`, `bb  |  22.250|`) and returns `("3.14", "   42|42   |00042", "a and b", "1.234568e+04", "ff 10", "+42", "      ab|cd      |", " 12.3%")`.
